Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.429 align:start position:0%
 
can i ask you about the quarantine  situation we've had more countries added  today i mean  to be fair smaller countries but  nevertheless i mean belgium  it is a concern it raises questions of  course as to whether we're going to see  bigger  tourist destinations like france  involved in  in the next step here i mean  what is your understanding of the  situation because you've got to be  across this surely  this has a big economic impact  it does i think look it's it's a tricky  situation what i can say to people  is we're in the midst of a global  pandemic and that means there is always  the risk of disruption to travel plans  and people need to bear that in mind  it's the right thing though for us to do  to keep  everything under review on a constant  basis talking with our scientists our  medical  advisors and if we need to take action  as you've seen overnight we will of  course  not hesitate to do that and we're doing  that to protect people's health  um but in the meantime you know people  should just continue to  to look at the guidance and make a  decision and take  everything into account themselves and  make a decision that they think is best  you're out and about today talking about  the amount of money that government has  has thrown into the project to try and  keep everything ticking along as  as best you can it do you get frustrated  when obviously there has to be a balance  between health  and economic health i mean this is a  a real step backwards isn't it when we  you know you  you do what you can but then we're sort  of really crippling the tourist industry  again  well i think we put a lot of uh support  in place to help the tourist industry  drive our recovery  uh out of this crisis i'm in scotland  today you know scotland is one of our  power brands when it comes  to tourism in the united kingdom i want  scotland to play a key part of our  recovery  so if you look at the vat reduction  we've put in place for the tourism  hospitality sector initiatives like eat  out  to help out you know all of those things  help protect the two million  plus jobs of people who work in that  industry  and you know i think we can slowly see  the benefits of that on the ground  um so i think we are actually making  steps forward with the support we put in  place  where are we in terms of things like  bounce back loans i know that's been one  of the  uh one of the the big sort of flag  waving events i know for a lot of  companies  will very much have welcomed it but  there is concern that  for other companies and smaller  companies it's just adding to an  ever-growing debt  and and some reports saying well up to  40 percent of those bounce-back loans  could never be paid back i know there's  lots of different reports uh out there  on on different estimates and what you  know what we do know is over  a million businesses give or take have  been able to receive bounce back loans  on average about 30 000 pounds they've  done in a way that should be  measured it's about a quarter of annual  turnover so it's affordable for them and  for the first year  no one has to pay anything back and and  after that uh there's a five-year period  for people to pay the loans back the  interest rate is very attractive at two  and a half  percent so we've designed these loans  particularly to to really help support  our small businesses  they've made i think a really  significant difference to businesses up  and down the country  helping them get through that difficult  period of the last few months when many  of them  were told to close but now that they're  reopened in a covered secure way  and they can look forward and that's why  it's important slowly as our economy  returns to normal we return to doing the  things  that we all enjoy doing and by doing  that we can  you know we can make sure that we try  and protect the millions of jobs that  are involved in all those industries  and realize that the new normal is one  that is safe you know we can  combat the virus continue to do things  in a way that is covered secure  but also get our economy uh back to  normal again and then in doing so  help protect as many jobs as possible  but when when you plan through all of  these sort of things  and heaven knows it must be incredibly  difficult  but you know talking about 30 000 pounds  on average but it's adding up to  billions upon billions of pounds  in total i mean what sort of percentage  of that have you  set aside to say right we're not going  to get this back  actually the office for national  statistics and others will make a  uh an evaluation of that and make an  adjustment for that on our account  i remain optimistic that if we can  actually drive our economic recovery  forward  then you know we we should be able to  recover a large amount of the loans that  we have  put out there the single best way to do  that is to make sure we get our economy  going  again but you know are some of those  loans going to be written off absolutely  we've  been very clear we won't be able to save  every single job every single business  despite the unprecedented amount of  support that we've put in place  but looking forward with our plan for  jobs tourism is a part of that and the  various other measures  we've put in place for example the  kickstart scheme to support  young people who are at risk of being  unemployed into new jobs supporting  apprenticeships for example  you know all those initiatives i think  will make a difference and help ensure  that as we  exit the acute phase of this crisis you  know we can slowly rebuild our economy  if people regain the confidence they had  and i think that that is the most  important thing for us all now to focus  on  look i i don't think anyone could  underestimate  how difficult to position you put in and  that would apply to you know  who was in government at the time of  something like this  but you're now faced with a situation  where i mean for example  something like the furlough scheme which  undoubtedly has been a lifeline to a lot  of people but now  who do you listen to moving forward  because there are some people  saying the furlough scheme has and is  protecting  unproductive jobs whilst others are  saying  the phasing out of the furlough scheme  is going to put  tens if not hundreds of thousands of  people out of work  who do you listen to when moving forward  on a scheme like that  we're right it's it's one of the most  difficult decisions that i've had to  make in this job  but if you look at it from start to  finish stephen  the government will have been stepping  in to help pay people's wages for almost  eight months which is an extraordinarily  long  period of time and i think most  reasonable people would look at that and  say  that's not something that is obviously  sustainable in the long run and  in common with many other countries  around the world their versions of this  are all coming to an end towards the end  of this year  that said we've still got support in  place the jobs retention bonus  will mean that for every employee that a  company is able to bring back from  furlough successfully employ them  through to january of next year they  will receive a thousand pounds bonus  which actually for the wages of most  people who have been furloughed will  make a significant difference especially  to those smaller companies  but i don't think it's fair to extend  this indefinitely it's not third of the  people on it we shouldn't  pretend that there is in every case a  job for someone to go back to  we actually should be helping them  prepare for their new opportunity  that they they should be looking for and  that's what our plan for jobs does and  i'm seeing that in action today in  scotland  but providing support for new  apprenticeships new training  jobs in the tourism industry this is  what we need to do now it's to look  forward  provide the opportunities for tomorrow  yes there is hardship ahead  for many people we know that but they  shouldn't be left without hope and i  think our plan for jobs provides that  hope  and that's what we can now do together  as we look forward to the future then  hopefully the economy picking up and  things getting back to  a new normal of some description or  other  when you sit down with a calculator at  night what sort of figures are we  totaling here i mean  what is what is the the cost of this so  far  right i do sit there with my calculator  at night um it  it's gonna this year we will borrow a  significant amount  of money to help pay for the various  measures that we've put in place i do  believe that's the right thing to do and  most independent economic commentators  agree with that as well  in a situation like this we should act  uh with boldness  to try and preserve as much of what we  call the productive capacity of our  economy for the long run  you know that said without question the  virus has taken a toll on our economy  and that will have a long run impact  over the next few years  our economy will likely be a bit smaller  than it otherwise would have been  and that obviously has implications for  you know what we can afford to do  our public finances have taken a  significant hit and over the medium term  we'll obviously need  to put our public finances on a  sustainable footing we can't continue  uh to operate in a way that we've done  this year in crisis mode  and that will require some difficult  decisions down the line and  i'm obviously prepared to make those  difficult decisions i think there will  be the right things to do  for the country i think all of us want  to see you know our strong economy  restored and that's the plan that we'll  set out  at the appropriate time  look we're out of time so i'm being  yelled at by my producers as you can  imagine but i've got to ask you i mean  instability and uncertainty are the two  key things  the econo the economy doesn't like um  can the economy cope in the next 24  months or so with  the effects of the pandemic and what  looks like a no deal brexit next year  well i think on on brexit as you would  have heard recent reports you know we  remain confident that it's possible to  get a deal  in september i think we've made good  progress on  lots of different areas in the  negotiations on trading goods  energy on transport social security  cooperation the structure of the deal  so there's been lots of good progress  you can see that it's possible that said  there do remain some considerable gaps  uh notified on things like fisheries uh  and so  there's there's work to do but we remain  very committed to working through those  remaining issues with our european  friends and allies and hopefully we can  conclude a deal but regardless one way  or another we will have certainty  and as you've seen from our  announcements elsewhere today on  helping businesses in northern ireland  we're making preparations  uh so that we can embrace our future  with confidence

